<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guan ju</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">seventh century BC</span> · Also known as 關雎, Guān jū</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Guan ju&quot; is the first poem of the Shi Jing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, and one of the best-known poems in Chinese literature. It has been dated to the seventh century BCE, which makes it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s oldest poems, though not the oldest in the anthology.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem opens the section Zhou nan of the Airs of the States, which make up 160 of the anthology's 305 poems, and like most of them it has three stanzas of four-character lines. Its title comes from the first line, the cry of ospreys on an islet in a river. At heart it tells of finding a good and fair young lady as a match for a young noble.</p><p>Each stanza sets a natural image, birds calling or water plants of varied length gathered left and right, beside a human scene without comment: the man longs for her, tosses and turns without getting her, and at last zithers, lutes, bells and drums greet her. Early commentators called this pairing of image and situation xing, one of the three rhetorical devices of the anthology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The earliest comment on it is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a>, which praises its joy without wantonness and sorrow without self-injury; Brooks and Brooks date that passage to 342 BCE. Confucian readers treated the poem as morally instructive. The Lu school read it as veiled criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-kang-of-zhou">King Kang of Zhou</a> and his wife, after the king was late for court one morning, and this view probably prevailed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>.</p><p>It was eclipsed by the Mao school, named after the annotators Mao Heng and Mao Chang, which read the poem as praise of Tai Si, queen of King Wen, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>. Zheng Xuan took the lady to be the palace women the queen sought for the king, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> thought the poem was written by ladies of the royal palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Other readers saw no allegory. A text found at Mawangdui in 1973, the Wuxingpian, treats it as a poem about regulating sexual desire, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> scholar Zheng Qiao called it an archaic folk song. James Legge, who translated it in 1871, rejected the Mao prefaces; Marcel Granet read it in 1911 as a song of a rural courting festival, and Edward L. Shaughnessy sees the fish-eating osprey as an image of the search for a mate.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yong">Cai Yong</a>, who died in 192, was among the first of many writers to allude to it. Phrases from it such as fair and good lady, seeking and not getting, and tossing and turning in bed survive as four-character idioms in modern Chinese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guan ju” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
